# Dependencies and build output
#
# No trailing slash on the first one: `node_modules/` matches a directory and
# nothing else, and this branch had already committed a *symlink* by that name
# — which the pattern let straight through. See the commit that removed it.
node_modules
out/
dist/
release/
coverage/
.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo

# NOT ignored, deliberately: `build/`.
# electron-builder reads it as `buildResources` — the app icon and the macOS
# entitlements live there. Most stock Node .gitignore templates carry a
# `build/` line, and pasting one in here silently ships an app with no icon.

# Secrets. Nothing below should ever reach a public repository.
.env
.env.*
.npmrc

# Signing and notarisation material. None of it exists yet — the build is
# unsigned — but an Apple Developer identity arrives as exactly these files,
# and the moment to have this rule is before one is downloaded, not after.
*.p12
# A Windows code-signing certificate arrives as a .pfx. Listed before one
# ever lands here, not after.
*.pfx
*.pem
*.key
*.cer
*.certSigningRequest
*.mobileprovision
*.p8

# Local agent configuration. Anything shared in `.claude/` may be committed;
# `settings.local.json` is the per-machine one, and it records local permission
# grants and absolute paths.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Where `scripts/remote-host.sh` keeps its bundle, this Mac's relay identity and
# the trust store it pairs phones into. Machine state, and one of those files is
# a private key.
#
# The glob, not just the bare name: `--name b` gives a second host a directory of
# its own — that is what makes two of them two *machines* rather than one started
# twice — and proving multi-host means running at least two. A pattern that only
# covered the default would have committed the second machine's private key.
.harness/.remote-host*/

# The same bundle, built to a folder of its own by the two-host recipe at the top
# of `ios/UITests/MultiHostUITests.swift`. Half a megabyte of esbuild output.
.harness/.multi-host/

# The GUEST side of the same harness — and the gap the rules above did not cover.
#
# The comment on `.remote-host*/` is proud of catching the second machine's
# private key. It did. Then the guest stand-in wrote its own keypair and its own
# pairing credential to a directory with no leading dot, so none of these
# patterns saw it, and `.harness/guest/device-key.json` — a 32-byte X25519
# private key — was committed to a PUBLIC repository, along with the credential
# naming the host it had paired to.
#
# What saved it was luck rather than design: the host it named was an ephemeral
# `--fresh` stand-in that no longer exists, so the leaked pair authorises nothing
# today. The next run against a real desktop would not have been so harmless.
#
# Ignore the artefacts by name rather than the folder, because `guest.ts` and
# `guest.mjs` are the harness itself and belong in the repo.
.harness/guest/device-key.json
.harness/guest/credential.txt
.harness/**/device-key.json
.harness/**/credential.txt

# Screenshots the harness writes. Evidence, not source: a UI change is not done
# until it has been rendered and looked at, which produces megabytes of PNGs per
# pass and none of them are worth a diff.
.harness/.shots/

# Editors
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# macOS
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes

# Windows
Thumbs.db

# Local notes on references consulted; never published (see CLAUDE.md).
REFERENCES.local.md

# Artefacts from `scripts/check-browser-drive.mjs` — a scratch Chromium profile,
# a bundle and a folder of screenshots, written when it is run with no --out.
.drive-check/
.vercel
